# Sizes here are asserted, not merely reported.
#
# `check-content.ts` fails the build when this asset's on-disk size differs from
# the value `build-content-catalog.ts` measured — an equality, not a tolerance —
# because that number is what the app advertises as the download.
#
# With no rules in this file, a Windows checkout under `core.autocrlf=true` —
# exactly what a fresh GitHub Actions runner has — rewrote all 75,695 line
# endings to CRLF and measured 8,760,835 bytes where the repository holds
# 8,685,140: the same words, a different environment, and a red build that no
# content change explains.
#
# `eol=lf` pins both ends of the round trip. Git restores LF on every checkout,
# so the asset measures the same bytes on any machine, and it folds any CRLF
# back to LF on the next commit, so a contributor whose editor wrote CRLF cannot
# record an inflated size that a later checkout fails to reproduce. Without the
# second half the check would only be half-fixed — the person running the
# catalogue build is the one who would break it next.
public/hadith/*.json text eol=lf

# Everything else stays on git's own auto-detection. `quran-full.json` is already
# classified binary (no line endings to convert) and is measured only against a
# 2 MB ceiling with headroom, so pinning the rest of the tree would change how
# hundreds of files check out in order to fix nothing.
